<Chapter id="bsn">
<h2>Sunken record stream</h2>
  <p>A Sunken service record begins with an 11-bit route. That route selects the
  payload schema whose fields determine where the record ends.</p>
  <p><a href="#bsn-walkthrough-title">The complete <code>Chat/2</code> Sunken record</a>
  shows the route, payload, padding, and boundary in two actual bytes.</p>

  <h3 id="route-header">Routing header</h3>
  <p>The first seven bits identify the command and distinguish service records from
  command responses. A service record then reads a four-bit service slot.</p>
  <RustSchema code={`record ServiceRoute {
    command_id: u6,
    service_present: u1 = 1,
    service_slot: u4,
}`} />
  <p>For a service record, the payload starts at bit 3 of the second byte.</p>
<pre is:raw><span class="syn-comment">for a route with a service slot</span>
<span class="syn-field">byte0</span> = (command &amp; <span class="syn-number">0x3f</span>) | <span class="syn-number">0x40</span> | (((slot &gt;&gt; <span class="syn-number">3</span>) &amp; <span class="syn-number">1</span>) &lt;&lt; <span class="syn-number">7</span>)
<span class="syn-field">byte1 bits 0..2</span> = slot &amp; <span class="syn-number">0x07</span>
</pre>

  <h3 id="bit-order">Bit order</h3>
  <p>The decoder keeps one <code>(byte index, bit index)</code> cursor for the entire
  record. Within each byte, it consumes bit 0 first and bit 7 last. After bit 7,
  the cursor continues at bit 0 of the next byte.</p>
  <p>A multi-bit value can cross that boundary. The first chunk becomes the
  higher-order part of the result, as the split four-bit service slot does in the
  walkthrough.</p>
<pre is:raw><span class="syn-keyword">read</span>(<span class="syn-field">width</span>):
    <span class="syn-field">value</span> = <span class="syn-number">0</span>
    <span class="syn-keyword">while</span> width remains:
        <span class="syn-field">take</span> = <span class="syn-keyword">min</span>(width, bits_left_in_current_byte)
        <span class="syn-field">chunk</span> = (current_byte &gt;&gt; current_bit_offset) &amp; ((<span class="syn-number">1</span> &lt;&lt; take) - <span class="syn-number">1</span>)
        <span class="syn-field">value</span> = (value &lt;&lt; take) | chunk
        <span class="syn-keyword">advance</span>(take)
</pre>

  <h4>Command response</h4>
  <p>When <code>service_present</code> is zero, the record is decoded as a fixed
  command response with a nine-bit result.</p>
  <RustSchema code={`record CommandResponse {
    command_id: u6,
    service_present: u1 = 0,
    result: u9,
}`} />
  <p>The command response consists of the command, service flag, and result. These
  fields total 16 bits and end on a byte boundary.</p>

  <h3 id="record-boundary">Record boundaries</h3>
  <p>A service record carries no total byte count. Its route selects a top-level
  type. Decoding that type advances the cursor to the logical end. Zero padding
  then advances the cursor to the next byte boundary.</p>

  <table>
    <caption>How a selected schema determines its own extent.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>Schema form</th><th>Bits consumed</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Fixed-width value</td><td>The width stored in BSN metadata.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Array, string, or blob</td><td>A local bounded count followed by that many elements or bytes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Optional value</td><td>A presence bit followed by the nested value when present.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Choice</td><td>A bounded selector followed by the selected arm.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Structure</td><td>Every member in its wire layout, including filler runs.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>A count uses the bounded-integer encoding. For a range
  <code>[minimum..maximum]</code>, metadata gives the bit width. The wire stores
  <code>value - minimum</code>. For example, <code>[1..64]</code> stores
  <code>count - 1</code> in six bits. A fixed range consumes zero count bits.</p>

  <p>The general calculation is:</p>
<pre is:raw><span class="syn-field">logical_bits</span> = <span class="syn-field">routing_header_bits</span> + <span class="syn-field">payload_bits_consumed_by_schema</span>
<span class="syn-field">padding_bits</span> = (-logical_bits) &amp; <span class="syn-number">7</span>
<span class="syn-field">record_bytes</span> = (logical_bits + padding_bits) / <span class="syn-number">8</span></pre>

  <p>An incremental decoder applies the selected schema directly to buffered bytes.
  If a field is incomplete, the decoder reads more TCP data. After the schema ends,
  it validates zero padding. The next routing header starts at the next byte.</p>

  <h4>Parsing</h4>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the six-bit command and one-bit service flag.</li>
    <li>For a command response, read its nine-bit result and finish the two-byte record.</li>
    <li>For a service record, read the four-bit slot.</li>
    <li>Resolve <code>(direction, slot, command)</code> to a top-level type.</li>
    <li>Decode that type, applying a generated wire layout where one exists.</li>
    <li>Require zero bits through the next byte boundary. The following byte begins the next record.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="notice"><b>Unknown service routes.</b> The payload schema is normally
  necessary to calculate the boundary. The current parser skips <code>Prty/0</code>
  as 18 bytes and slot 13 command 50 as 38 bytes. Any other unknown route stops
  decoding.</div>

  <p>Internal alignment applies to the complete record. A string, byte string, or
  blob can align the cursor before its raw bytes. Encode the route and payload with
  one cursor beginning at bit zero of the complete record.</p>
  <p><a href="#services">The static service map</a> assigns each implemented slot.
  <a href="#metadata">The metadata section</a> defines the selected payload type.</p>
  <BackToContents />
</Chapter>
